WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
In previous videos I've talked about a&nbsp;
lot of different aiming misconceptions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.480
and most of then tend to start and end&nbsp;
with the idea of instant improvement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.880
And the conclusion to all of those videos has&nbsp;
been that there is no cheat code to aiming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.200
and that it just comes down to putting in the time&nbsp;
needed to develop your skills. But then, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.360
can't optimise outside factors can you optimise&nbsp;
the time you put in instead? Is there a most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.360
efficient way to train your aim and if there is,&nbsp;
what is it and how far can we narrow this down?

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.200
If you haven't already heard of&nbsp;
them, aimtrainers are that most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.200
efficient method. When compared to trying&nbsp;
to train your aim by just playing the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.480
the amount of time you spend actually aiming&nbsp;
is way higher in an aimtrainer meaning you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.080
more practice in for your time and on top of&nbsp;
that, the ridiculous number of scenarios gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.800
far more options when it comes to working&nbsp;
on weaknesses in your aim when compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.960
to any in game training tool. Even if these&nbsp;
scenarios aren't 1:1 with a specific game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:59.440
aimtrainers are still the best way to develop good&nbsp;
technique, which will carry over any fps game.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.720
This probably isn't new info for you, but&nbsp;
something I haven't heard answered as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:09.080
is the how? How do we actually isolate and&nbsp;
improve upon weaknesses? How can you tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.560
what your weaknesses even are, and what's&nbsp;
with all this vague discussion about good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.000
technique? My goal with this video is to go&nbsp;
through everything you need to know to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.440
into aimtraining and how to get the most out&nbsp;
of it, I'm hoping to write this in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.000
will naturally follow players progression and&nbsp;
the info will build on what's said prior but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.680
if you are more experienced and already&nbsp;
know the basics, don't be afraid to skip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.160
ahead. for now though, lets start at the&nbsp;
start. Which aimtrainer should you choose?

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.760
There are two main options I'd recommend&nbsp;
to beginners, Aimlabs and Kovaaks. Kovaaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.320
has better scenarios that cover a wider range&nbsp;
of skills and I strongly recommend it if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.280
main game you play is apex legends&nbsp;
for a reason I'll get into later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.160
but aimlabs is free. If you aren't sure you want&nbsp;
to spend a lot of time aimtraining I recommend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:58.200
starting with aimlabs and seeing if you stick&nbsp;
with it and if you do swap over to kovaaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.640
if you are going to put the time in&nbsp;
it's well worth the asking price.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.760
So, you've got your aim trainer, what now?

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.960
The first step is to get some nice&nbsp;
settings, I've linked two videos below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.320
both by Mattyow where he talks about his&nbsp;
kovaaks and aimlabs settings. How much this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.440
matters for improvement is debatable&nbsp;
but personally I find it a lot easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.280
to commit to playing aimtrainers&nbsp;
with nice hitsounds and visuals.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.600
After that I'd recommend trying the voltaic&nbsp;
benchmarks. These aren't an all in one training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.320
tool but they do provide some good scenarios&nbsp;
to start with however more importantly they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.120
give you an idea of where your skills stand&nbsp;
relative to each other. This is the easiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.320
way to identify weaknesses when you're starting&nbsp;
out and I think when you are below diamond on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:46.400
these benchmarks it's worth trying to keep&nbsp;
all your skills at mostly the same level. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.160
if you have any categories or scenarios that&nbsp;
are significantly lower than everything else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.800
try to bring those up to the rank of your best&nbsp;
skills. This is because even though some skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.560
are more applicable to some games than others,&nbsp;
at all levels but especially first starting out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.200
the concentrated practice that any of these&nbsp;
scenarios provide will benefit you in game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.320
even if right now it might not be&nbsp;
obvious how these things link together.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.080
If you're on aimlab you can find the benchmarks&nbsp;
on the voltaic website, click assess your aim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.840
and try out the scenarios, it will automatically&nbsp;
fill out your scores as you play. This is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.480
to be done with kovaaks benchmarks at some point&nbsp;
but for now you can find them this spreadsheet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.480
you fill your scores in here as you play&nbsp;
each task or you can use the auto updater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.680
tool which does this for you, all three&nbsp;
of these are linked in the description.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.000
I would recommend only playing the&nbsp;
benchmarks maybe 2 times a week at most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.160
they are good for testing yourself and, especially&nbsp;
when you're first getting into aimtraining they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.840
will help you improve but I still think most&nbsp;
of your time should be spent practicing. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.680
practice comes in multiple forms, how I approach&nbsp;
this personally is something I'll cover in a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.800
but as a baseline when starting out, if you want&nbsp;
to aimtrain every day I would recommend spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.840
3 days playing playlists that target weak areas&nbsp;
in your benchmarks, 2 days playing either the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.920
fundamental or game specific routines for your&nbsp;
game of choice and the other 2 days benchmarking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:12.280
but this is just a guideline. I've also linked a&nbsp;
bunch of both game specific and weakness focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:17.240
routines by myself and others in the description&nbsp;
as starting points, but these are just guidelines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:22.800
as well. As your main weaknesses or main games&nbsp;
change you should change routines but you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.840
customise them further, if you think a routine is&nbsp;
too long for how much time you have or too hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.400
for your current skill level, don't be afraid&nbsp;
to remove scenarios or change scenarios for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.400
easier versions, it's ok to change these things&nbsp;
to suit your preference or time restrictions.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.000
There is a more important discussion to be had&nbsp;
when it comes to time management and aimtraining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.640
though, which is how you should balance your&nbsp;
time spent in the game versus aimtraining.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:52.120
This varies so much it's hard for me to even&nbsp;
give a rough estimate but it depends on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.160
experience and game of choice. The less&nbsp;
experienced you are on mouse and keyboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.280
the bigger the benefits of aimtraining will&nbsp;
be since an hour of aimtraining for a person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.800
who started playing on pc yesterday is&nbsp;
a much larger % of their total playtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.880
than an hour of aimtraining would&nbsp;
be for me. Because of that it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:12.000
easier to justify playing more aimtraining&nbsp;
relative to others when you're brand new.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.040
The other big factor is which game you&nbsp;
play, Valorant and most other tactical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.560
shooters forces its players to get&nbsp;
very good at one specific skill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:26.280
microadjustments. There are exceptions but you can&nbsp;
win a lot of gunfights in that game by just being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:32.320
able to make small movements accurately. Compare&nbsp;
this to apex or other high time to kill games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.160
where it's wider weapon variety and higher time&nbsp;
to kill force more consistent and varied aim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:42.280
over a longer period, placing a higher demand&nbsp;
on raw mechanical skill. This means that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.800
benefits of aimtraining will shine through more&nbsp;
in apex, especially scenarios like controlsphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.920
whisphere and air which not only feel very&nbsp;
analogous to tracking in that game but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:57.200
also develop very important fundamental skills&nbsp;
and basically force good technique which will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.520
help you in game. This is also why I recommend&nbsp;
kovaaks so strongly for apex players by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.200
aimlab doesn't do scenarios like this&nbsp;
nearly as well. These scenarios will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.680
help in valorant as well, but only for&nbsp;
the fundamentals and skills they build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.000
the movements don't directly translate, put&nbsp;
a pin in this, I'll come back to it later.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.360
So those are the two main factors, skill level and&nbsp;
game of choice. Personally though I'd put an upper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.920
limit of spending 20% of your daily fps playtime&nbsp;
in an aimtrainer. Past this, your typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:32.120
getting into the territory of diminishing returns&nbsp;
and that time is better spent playing the game.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:37.320
A bit ago I mentioned technique, and this is a&nbsp;
topic with so much depth I can't cover it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:42.600
in this video but I think it can be underpinned&nbsp;
with a single idea, making your mouse movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.840
more efficient. This, on it's own is somewhat&nbsp;
meaningless but it should become a bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.120
valuable after going through some of the aiming&nbsp;
categories and seeing how it applies within them.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:58.160
Static clicking is, on its surface, the&nbsp;
simplest category. And when I first saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.840
a scenario like this I thought, how is&nbsp;
this useful? It's just dots on a wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.840
and it yet it's invaluable for developing&nbsp;
and refining your flicks. When playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.360
try to make a fast initial flick which lands close&nbsp;
to the target before making a smaller movement to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.360
correct for any errors made during your first.&nbsp;
The simplicity of static is its strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:26.040
these two core movements, the flick and micro&nbsp;
correction, appear everywhere in more advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.840
forms but by stripping all of that fluff&nbsp;
away you can isolate the underlying motions.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:36.280
To link it back to that key idea there are 2 ways&nbsp;
you should try to optimise your mouse movements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:40.880
For both initial flicks and microadjustments&nbsp;
you want to try and draw as straight of a line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.040
as possible between your starting point and the&nbsp;
target. Curving or having jagged lines is a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.720
common issue and something to try and smooth out&nbsp;
sooner rather than later even if it comes at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.360
cost of initial flick speed. The second thing to&nbsp;
focus on is where your flick lands, obviously dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:02.200
on is ideal but this isn't always possible and&nbsp;
underflicking is far preferable to overflicking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:06.120
This is because of the change in the direction&nbsp;
of the force your applying when you overflick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:11.520
and have to correct backwards, it's a lot harder&nbsp;
than just following through with the same motion.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.760
This concept is even more applicable to&nbsp;
tracking, it just applies in different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.720
ways. One of the most common issues I see in&nbsp;
tracking is overadjusting after the target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:26.400
changes directions. Both overshooting and landing&nbsp;
on the far side of the target are suboptimal here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.160
since even if you do land on target you are&nbsp;
still moving your mouse more than you have to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.040
increasing the amount of distance the target&nbsp;
appears to travel relative to your crosshair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.120
making their movements harder to read. This often&nbsp;
leads to chaining these overadjustments together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:45.080
which leads to this feeling of always lagging&nbsp;
behind what the targets actually doing. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:50.080
easiest way to avoid this is to try to land&nbsp;
on the middle or inner edge of the target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.240
by minimising you mouse movement you make&nbsp;
it easier to read the target's and when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.960
changes directions again you'll remain on&nbsp;
it for longer before having to readjust.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:04.040
Dynamic clicking almost blends both of these&nbsp;
because it requires clean flicks to first reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.600
the target, clean microadjustments when needed&nbsp;
and good movement reading skills to tell where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:12.240
the target is going to end up after you flick to&nbsp;
it. Having straight lines and not overshooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.800
helps with movement reading as well, combining&nbsp;
the skills needed in the previous 2 categories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.960
and again, minimising mouse movement is&nbsp;
what is needed to have good technique in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.350
all 3. Again there is so much more to the topic&nbsp;
but I hope looking at technique through this lens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:22.437
can help clarify things, I'll make a video&nbsp;
discussing this in more depth in the future.

00:09:22.437 --> 00:09:26.520
So that's most of the key information needed&nbsp;
to get started and get a lot of value out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.040
aimtraining but what if this isn't working for&nbsp;
you? Maybe you've reached a plateau and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.880
looking for a new way to practice. And I think&nbsp;
this will happen to everyone at some point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:40.040
routines are good but can only&nbsp;
get you so far and in my opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.280
past a certain rank you need to look&nbsp;
into more self directed training.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:48.840
What I do personally when training for a&nbsp;
specific fps game is, I'll play aimtrainers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.680
for maybe 15 minutes as a warmup beforehand.&nbsp;
After my playsession, I'll aimtrain again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:59.520
for another 30-45 minutes but during that time,&nbsp;
I'll focus more specifically on issues I noticed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.040
while playing. For example, if I'm playing&nbsp;
the finals and I notice that I'm somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:08.960
shaky when trying to track with my arm, after&nbsp;
my playsession I'd play some smoothbot to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.560
and address that issue instead of doing&nbsp;
any weakness specific routines. The caveat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:17.200
with this is that you have to be able&nbsp;
to pick out your issues very quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.680
vod reviewing might be useful if you can't do&nbsp;
it in real time but this is something I'd only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:26.760
recommend trying if you have hit that plateau&nbsp;
and want to try and change up your approach

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.280
But what is the benefit of this? Why would I&nbsp;
choose my own scenarios instead of choosing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:35.800
routine containing scenarios that are meant to do&nbsp;
the same thing? The answer to this is threefold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:41.520
a minor reason is that I personally find I improve&nbsp;
faster if I change up what I'm doing consistently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:45.880
picking out scenarios to play by hand lets me do&nbsp;
that far better than swapping between different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:51.400
routines. The next is that routines can sometimes&nbsp;
lead to autopiloting, when you just hop on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:57.520
play your routine and hop off. This is fine and&nbsp;
still will help but I think forcing myself to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.400
mindful about what I'm playing and why I'm playing&nbsp;
it helps give myself direction and prevents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:07.880
aimtraining from just becoming a habit, it's&nbsp;
focused practice instead. Which leads well into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:13.000
the third point, picking scenarios specifically&nbsp;
gives me far more control over the level of focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:18.640
I want to place on a certain skill. Even the best&nbsp;
routines unless they are made for you can't target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:23.160
all of your weak points all of the time but by&nbsp;
doing this, I can make good on that initial pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:28.800
for aimtraining and isolate issues as specifically&nbsp;
as possible and improve on them faster.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.280
The two hardest parts of this more self&nbsp;
directed approach is figuring out what your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:38.080
weaknesses are at a more specific level and&nbsp;
finding the right scenarios to address them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:42.920
so lets tackle those in order. When vod reviewing&nbsp;
your aimtrainer gameplay, try to find a top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:48.360
run of that scenario and compare and contrast&nbsp;
your run with theirs. To be specific, look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:53.000
the differences in your approach and what skills&nbsp;
they are leaning into to play it the way they do.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:57.600
I think this is best explained with an example,&nbsp;
so I'm going to compare my static runs with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:03.480
previous top static player with a similar style&nbsp;
to my own, trustemix. When watching these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:07.840
you can see that we have a similar initial&nbsp;
accuracy for smaller flicks and I might even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:12.760
be better at these but over wider angles he&nbsp;
both lands closer to the dot and corrects to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:17.400
it quicker. This is because most of the time&nbsp;
when he does miss the initial flick he stops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:22.120
before reaching the target and all he needs&nbsp;
to do is make a small correction in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:27.840
direction. Compare this to my run, I have more&nbsp;
directional inaccuracy, my lines curve over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:33.000
wide distances so I have to correct sideways&nbsp;
which takes longer. So, I don't need to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:38.400
on my closer flicks for now and should instead&nbsp;
focus on cleaning up my lines on my wider ones.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:45.640
This method still works with a wider skill gap&nbsp;
between you and the person you're comparing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:51.320
as well, even if you can't isolate a specific&nbsp;
thing that you're worse at you can identify how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:56.040
they are approaching the scenario and figure&nbsp;
out where you differ. For another example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.560
my girlfriend Jadey has gotten into&nbsp;
aimtraining recently and she watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:05.240
Matty's vods to learn from even though she was&nbsp;
several ranks lower. On bounce 180 specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:09.000
she noticed that he would mainly focus on&nbsp;
targets towards the centre of the map and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:13.120
often killed them right after they spawned,&nbsp;
whereas she would focus on all targets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.920
equally and because of that lost a lot of time&nbsp;
going for ones near the corners. By addressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:23.040
this core issue she was then able to focus on&nbsp;
improving specific things like leading shots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:28.000
and building up speed over time instead of&nbsp;
being stuck because of an incorrect approach.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:33.800
But how do I know what scenarios to play to&nbsp;
actually do that? There is a list of recommended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:38.960
scenarios for kovaaks and aimlabs by voltaic,&nbsp;
which has heaps of things to try out sorted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:42.840
category which should help narrow them down to&nbsp;
what you're looking for. But if you want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:47.760
more specific, try to play variants of scenarios&nbsp;
you know of by searching for the keyword of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:53.440
